      <tr><th scope="row">Training and development coordinator</th><td>Corporate L&amp;D departments, nonprofits</td><td>Bachelor's; master's preferred</td><td>Entry-to-mid-level professional pay</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p>Salary figures come from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook and Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (May 2023 data). They describe typical pay, not guarantees, and actual earnings depend on location, employer, and experience. The sections below explain what each career path involves and what it takes to enter it.</p>

  <h2 id="research-roles">What are research roles like?</h2>
  <p>Research is the field's home turf, and it hires at every degree level. Bachelor's graduates run studies as research assistants. Master's graduates lead projects as analysts or lab managers. PhDs set the agenda as principal investigators and research scientists. Settings range from universities and government labs to corporate R&amp;D.</p>
  <p>Research is the heart of behavioral psychology, and it employs people at every degree level. As a research assistant with a bachelor's degree, you help run studies: recruiting participants, collecting data, and doing preliminary analysis. With a master's, you can lead projects as a research analyst or lab manager, designing studies and publishing findings. A PhD opens the role of principal investigator or research scientist, where you set the research agenda and compete for grant funding.</p>
  <p>Research roles exist in universities, independent research institutes, government agencies, and corporate R&amp;D labs. The work is methodical and rewarding if you enjoy asking precise questions and letting data answer them. Funded research runs on grants from agencies like the National Science Foundation and the National Institutes of Health, so grant-writing skill directly affects a research scientist's career. The main trade-off is pay at the entry level, which tends to be modest; advancement and funding improve considerably with a graduate degree.</p>

  <h2 id="education-roles">What education and academia roles exist?</h2>
  <p>University teaching almost always requires a PhD plus published research, with time split between teaching, mentoring, and running a lab. Below that level, master's graduates work as lecturers, advisors, and instructional designers. In corporate education, training coordinators use learning theory to build onboarding programs.</p>
  <p>Teaching at the university level almost always requires a PhD, along with a record of published research. Professors split their time between teaching, mentoring students, and running research programs. Below the professor level, master's graduates find work as lecturers, academic advisors, and instructional designers who apply behavioral principles to how courses are structured.</p>
  <p>Outside higher education, behavioral training is useful in corporate education and nonprofit program design. Training and development coordinators, for example, use learning theory to build onboarding programs and measure whether employees actually change behavior after training. These roles usually ask for a bachelor's degree, with a master's preferred for senior positions.</p>

  <h2 id="business-roles">What business and industry roles hire behavioral psychology graduates?</h2>
  <p>Business is where many graduates build well-paid careers without a doctorate. Industrial-organizational psychology is the flagship: applying behavioral science to hiring, team performance, and workplace design. The Society for Industrial and Organizational Psychology (SIOP) is the field's professional home, and its annual conference is a major hiring venue. UX research, people analytics, and organizational development are the other big doors, mostly at the master's level.</p>
  <p>Business is where many behavioral psychology graduates build well-paid careers without a doctorate. Industrial-organizational psychology is the flagship field here: applying behavioral science to hiring, team performance, leadership development, and workplace design. The BLS reports a median wage of $147,420 for industrial-organizational psychologists (May 2023), though this reflects a small, highly credentialed occupation and individual results vary.</p>
  <p>Related master's-level roles include organizational behavior specialist, people analytics consultant, and program evaluator. At the bachelor's level, human resources analyst and market research roles use the same core skills: understanding motivation, designing surveys, and interpreting behavioral data. UX research deserves special mention: tech companies hire psychology graduates to study how users behave with products, and the pay is competitive, though it varies widely by company and seniority. A master's degree and a portfolio of research projects strengthen any of these applications.</p>

  <h2 id="health-roles">What health and human services roles fit?</h2>
  <p>Behavioral psychology shows up in health through treatment adherence, public health campaigns, and program evaluation. Health behavior specialists and program evaluators usually hold a master's. Clinical and counseling psychologists need a doctorate and state licensure; a general behavioral psychology degree alone does not confer it. The applied field's main professional credential is the BCBA (Board Certified Behavior Analyst), which requires a master's, verified coursework, supervised fieldwork, and an exam.</p>
  <p>Behavioral psychology plays a growing role in health: helping patients stick to treatment plans, designing public health campaigns, and evaluating community programs. Health behavior specialists and program evaluators work in hospitals, public health agencies, and nonprofits, usually with a master's degree. Clinical and counseling psychologists, who need a doctorate and state licensure, provide assessment and therapy; the BLS reports their median pay at $96,100 (May 2023).</p>
  <p>An important note on scope: this site is educational. If you are drawn to health roles, understand that providing diagnosis or treatment requires licensed clinical training, which a general behavioral psychology degree alone does not confer. Plan your degree path with licensure requirements in mind from the start.</p>

  <h2 id="skills-employers-value">Which skills do employers value most?</h2>
  <p>Across all four sectors, hiring managers look for the same cluster of skills. The degree gives you the credential; these abilities get you the job: research design, statistics, data tools, clear writing, an understanding of motivation and learning, and practical experience from internships or lab work.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research design and statistics.</strong> The ability to design a clean study and analyze the results correctly is the degree's signature skill.</li>
    <li><strong>Data tools.</strong> Familiarity with statistical software and spreadsheets, and increasingly with programming for data analysis, sets candidates apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear writing.</strong> Turning findings into reports, proposals, and presentations that non-specialists can act on.</li>
    <li><strong>Understanding of motivation and learning.</strong> The behavioral core: reinforcement, habit formation, and how environments shape choices. See our <a href="/behavioral-psychology-examples/">everyday examples</a> to see these principles in action.</li>
    <li><strong>Practical experience.</strong> Internships, practicums, and research assistantships count heavily. Employers prefer candidates who have applied methods outside the classroom.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="job-outlook">What is the job outlook?</h2>
  <p>The US Bureau of Labor Statistics projects employment for psychologists to grow about 6 percent from 2023 to 2033, faster than the average for all occupations, driven by demand for psychological services in schools, hospitals, and organizations. Adjacent fields like UX research and organizational development show steady demand under different occupational categories.</p>
  <p>Outlook figures describe occupations, not individuals. Your results will depend on your degree level, practical experience, and willingness to follow the work into industries beyond traditional psychology settings. Graduates who combine the degree with data skills and real project experience tend to have the most options.</p>

  <h2 id="getting-started">How do you get started?</h2>
  <p>Match the career to the degree before you apply anywhere. The <a href="/degrees/">degrees hub</a> compares every level, with costs and timelines, so you can pick the path that unlocks the roles you want. If you already know your target level, go deeper: the <a href="/degrees/ms-behavioral-psychology/">MS guide</a> for applied master's careers, the <a href="/degrees/phd-in-behavioral-psychology/">PhD guide</a> for research and academia, or the <a href="/degrees/online-behavioral-psychology-degree/">online programs guide</a> if you need flexibility. And if you are still deciding whether the field fits, read <a href="/what-is-behavioral-psychology/">what behavioral psychology is</a> first. The right preparation now makes the job search later much simpler.</p>
